---

<Base title="The Experiment — The Sustainability Diet" description="A concrete proposal: a parcel of desertified ground bought distressed, one hundred camels, an osage orange orchard, and a five-year MVP into a ten-year flip window. The experiment the book's Epilogue asks someone to let us run." readingBar>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-reading px-6 pt-20 pb-32">

    <header class="mb-14">
      <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-4">The proposal</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl tracking-tight leading-tight mb-6">
        One hundred camels. An osage orange orchard.
        A measurement series that runs long enough to mean anything.
      </h1>
      <p class="font-serif text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed text-ink/75 max-w-reading">
        This page is the experiment the book is asking
        someone to let us run. If you have land, capital
        to underwrite a distressed-land acquisition, or the
        authority to grant permission, it was written for you.
      </p>
    </header>

    {/* Jump-nav — collapsed by default on mobile, expanded on desktop */}
    <details class="mb-14 border border-ink/20 bg-bone/30 print:hidden" open>
      <summary class="cursor-pointer select-none px-5 py-3 small-caps text-xs text-ochre-deep tracking-wide hover:bg-bone/60">
        Sixteen sections · jump to
      </summary>
      <nav class="px-5 pb-5 pt-1 border-t border-ink/10">
        <ol class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 gap-y-1">
          {toc.map(t => (
            <li>
              <a href={`#${t.id}`} class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 py-1 font-serif text-ink/80 hover:text-ink">
                <span class="small-caps text-[0.65rem] text-ochre-deep tabular-nums tracking-wide flex-shrink-0 w-6">
                  {t.n}
                </span>
                <span class="text-sm md:text-base leading-snug group-hover:underline decoration-ochre underline-offset-4">
                  {t.label}
                </span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </nav>
    </details>

    <div class="prose-reading text-lg leading-[1.7] text-ink/90 space-y-7">

      <!-- Section 1: The wish, said plainly -->
      <section id="s01" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">01</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          The wish, said plainly.
        </h2>

        <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-ochre pl-6 my-8 font-serif italic text-xl text-ink/85 max-w-reading">
          I would like, somewhere in my life, to see one hundred
          camels in an osage orange orchard.
          <footer class="mt-3 not-italic text-sm text-ink/50 small-caps">
            From the Prologue
          </footer>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          The Prologue ends on a sentence that is easy to read as a
          turn of phrase. It is not. It is a specification. A hundred
          camels because that is enough for a small, real herd. An
          osage orange orchard because those trees produce a fruit no
          modern animal on the continent will eat, and that fact is
          the cleanest available proof that something about the
          landscape has been broken for a very long time. The
          sentence names the animals, the plants, and the
          arrangement. What it leaves unsaid is the observation
          platform: a working site, a measurement protocol, and
          enough time to tell whether anything has changed.
        </p>

        <p>
          The rest of this page is what the sentence leaves unsaid.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: What flipping the ground means here -->
      <section id="s02" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">02</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          What &ldquo;flipping the ground&rdquo; means here.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The Epilogue names the register: <em>bought distressed,
          returned to function by putting the right animals back on
          it and the right trees in it, running as a closed-loop
          system for a decade with honest monitoring, and then sold,
          or kept, or left to the commons, whichever way the
          experiment wants to go once the experiment has been run.</em>
          That sentence is the frame this proposal is written in.
        </p>

        <p>
          A research-farm framing would ask a landowner with working
          ground to host a decade of experiments. This proposal does
          the opposite. It wants acreage that is <em>sick</em> at
          acquisition &mdash; thinning cover, bare ground above ten
          percent, declining carbon, priced down from comparable
          ground for a reason. Desertifying ground is already losing
          value. The people running the experiment are the ones for
          whom that loss of value is the point. We want to know what
          a parcel like that becomes when a missing mouth is put back
          on it and enough calendar is allowed to pass.
        </p>

        <p>
          &ldquo;Flipped&rdquo; also names the thing a long-duration
          research project most needs and most lacks: an exit. At
          the close of the ten-year window, the parcel takes a
          disposition that has been defined in advance, not
          negotiated after the signal is read. There are four
          candidates, and a funder or landowner can commit to any
          one of them as the terminal condition of the experiment.
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 not-prose my-8 font-body">
          <div class="border border-ink/15 p-5 bg-bone/30">
            <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Disposition A</div>
            <div class="font-display text-base mb-2">Sold at restored valuation.</div>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed">
              If a private-capital syndicate underwrote the
              acquisition, this disposition returns the difference
              between the distressed basis and the restored value
              to the underwriters. The signal this produces to
              adjacent land markets is the one that matters most:
              restoration creates land value, and the market
              learns from that the way it learns from anything
              else.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-ink/15 p-5 bg-bone/30">
            <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Disposition B</div>
            <div class="font-display text-base mb-2">Retained and extended.</div>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed">
              If the closed-loop test indicates the hypothesis has
              held at this site, the parcel is held through Year 25
              for the long recruitment signal &mdash; do the
              camel-dispersed osage orange seedlings reach
              reproductive age and re-seed on their own? This is
              the disposition the researchers would prefer. It is
              also the most expensive.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-ink/15 p-5 bg-bone/30">
            <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Disposition C</div>
            <div class="font-display text-base mb-2">Easement-protected.</div>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed">
              The parcel is sold to an adjacent working landowner
              or a conservation buyer under a perpetual easement
              that preserves the closed-loop structure. The
              underwriters take a discounted return; the landscape
              keeps the intervention in place.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-ink/15 p-5 bg-bone/30">
            <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Disposition D</div>
            <div class="font-display text-base mb-2">Returned to the commons.</div>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed">
              Transferred to a tribal nation, a public land trust,
              or a permanent-steward non-profit. The underwriters
              take the loss; the restoration is treated as
              non-market. This is the disposition most consistent
              with the Epilogue&rsquo;s final line.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          The rest of this page describes the research. The
          research is what makes the flip meaningful. The flip
          frame is what makes the research fundable without a
          donor pipeline.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: The hypothesis -->
      <section id="s03" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">03</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          The hypothesis.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Desertification is usually framed as an environmental
          problem. The book reframes it as a disease. A landscape
          metabolizes; it takes in water, sun, seed, and forage,
          and it processes them through a set of biotic loops that
          include the mouths of the animals that live on it. Remove
          a load-bearing mouth and the loops do not simply downsize.
          They fail in specific ways. Fruit falls and rots where it
          fell. Shrubs encroach because nothing crops them. Forage
          over-matures and choke-mulches itself. Seeds wait in the
          wrong places for dispersers that are not coming. The
          familiar symptoms of desertification &mdash; thinning ground
          cover, compaction, reduced infiltration, lower soil carbon,
          hotter surface temperatures &mdash; follow.
        </p>

        <p>
          The predictive claim being tested: in a western North
          American landscape with documented Pleistocene
          <em>Camelops</em> occurrence, a browsing proxy at
          ecologically meaningful density, paired with targeted
          plantings of a plant family whose dispersers are missing,
          will produce measurable changes in soil carbon, water
          infiltration, seed dispersal, and vegetation composition
          on a decadal timescale, in directions consistent with the
          loop-restoration hypothesis.
        </p>

        <p class="font-serif text-ink/75 italic">
          The predictive claim is narrow on purpose. Running the
          experiment does not require settling the broader
          Pleistocene-rewilding debate. It requires a site, a herd,
          an orchard, a monitoring protocol, and enough calendar.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Why camels, why osage -->
      <section id="s04" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">04</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          Why camels. Why osage orange.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Camels are not gomphotheres. They are closer in body plan,
          gut size, and browsing habit to the missing mouths of the
          late-Pleistocene American West than any other large mammal
          currently available on the continent. Camelidae evolved
          in North America. Modern dromedaries and Bactrians are
          descended from North American emigrants that crossed into
          Eurasia via Beringia and diversified there. <em>Camelops
          hesternus</em>, the Western camel, is the closer kin of
          modern Old World camels than any living llama is.<sup><a href="#fn1" id="ref1">1</a></sup>
          The gap between a dromedary and a <em>Camelops</em> is real
          but smaller than the gap between any plausible alternative
          proxy and the animal that used to be here.
        </p>

        <p>
          Osage orange (<em>Maclura pomifera</em>) is the cleanest
          test organism in the anachronistic-fruit literature. The
          fruit is large, grainy, latex-bearing, inedible to
          essentially every extant North American vertebrate, and
          produced in quantity anyway. A tree with a bumper crop
          looks like a tree producing for no one. It is the
          observation Janzen and Martin built their 1982 paper
          around.<sup><a href="#fn2" id="ref2">2</a></sup> The question
          this experiment asks of the tree is the question Janzen
          and Martin asked of the literature: what does the fruit
          look like when it has a mouth again?
        </p>

        <p>
          Ancillary plantings extend the diagnostic. Honey locust
          (<em>Gleditsia triacanthos</em>) produces a long sugary
          pod with comparable orphan characteristics. Kentucky
          coffeetree (<em>Gymnocladus dioicus</em>) produces heavy
          toxic pods with an even more restricted extant disperser
          set.<sup><a href="#fn3" id="ref3">3</a></sup> Mesquite
          (<em>Prosopis</em> spp.) and several native <em>Acacia</em>
          are included because their pods are within the documented
          dietary range of modern camelids and are native to sites
          with realistic soil-water profiles for the target region.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: The family of work -->
      <section id="s05" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">05</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          The family of work this belongs to.
        </h2>

        <p>
          This experiment is not a first-of-kind in spirit. It
          belongs to a two-decade cluster of large-landscape
          restoration and megafaunal-proxy projects that have been
          run with various degrees of rigor and various theories
          of change. Situating the proposal inside that family is
          honest and useful: it clarifies what this project borrows,
          what it changes, and what it is deliberately not.
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 not-prose my-8 font-body">
          <div class="border border-ink/15 p-6 bg-bone/30">
            <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Adjacent project</div>
            <div class="font-display text-lg mb-2">American Prairie</div>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed">
              Montana. Assembling a three-million-acre grassland
              reserve on the northern Great Plains, reintroducing
              bison as the keystone grazer. The theory of change is
              land aggregation and ungulate-driven grassland
              restoration at continental scale.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/60 leading-relaxed mt-3">
              <em>What this borrows:</em> the commitment to
              long-duration monitoring and the willingness to work
              at landscape, not plot, scale.<br>
              <em>What this changes:</em> a deeper baseline. Bison
              is post-glacial; the loop this project is asking about
              pre-dates bison.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-ink/15 p-6 bg-bone/30">
            <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Adjacent project</div>
            <div class="font-display text-lg mb-2">Pleistocene Park</div>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed">
              Northeast Siberia. Sergey and Nikita Zimov's long-running
              mammoth-steppe restoration, using large herbivores as
              the tool. Target: permafrost stabilization through
              grazing-mediated albedo and soil-temperature change.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/60 leading-relaxed mt-3">
              <em>What this borrows:</em> the explicit use of a
              megafaunal proxy to test an ecological-feedback
              hypothesis, and the discipline of measuring soil-level
              outcomes rather than charismatic ones.<br>
              <em>What this changes:</em> the target is
              desertification, not permafrost. The climate regime,
              the plant community, the proxy animal, and the failure
              modes are all different.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-ink/15 p-6 bg-bone/30">
            <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Adjacent project</div>
            <div class="font-display text-lg mb-2">Armendaris Ranch</div>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed">
              New Mexico. Ted Turner's Chihuahuan Desert ranch, home
              to the long-running Bolson tortoise reintroduction
              (the first North American return of a Pleistocene
              survivor to its ecological range) and ongoing
              experimental work on desert grassland recovery under
              large-ungulate management.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/60 leading-relaxed mt-3">
              <em>What this borrows:</em> the private-land,
              long-horizon, single-operator governance model that
              makes decadal commitments possible.<br>
              <em>What this changes:</em> the experiment is not
              tortoise-scale. The question is whether a browsing
              proxy at ungulate body mass can reopen a specific
              plant&ndash;disperser loop.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-ink/15 p-6 bg-bone/30">
            <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Adjacent project</div>
            <div class="font-display text-lg mb-2">Oostvaardersplassen &amp; Knepp</div>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed">
              Netherlands and England. Two European rewilding
              experiments using cattle and horse lineages as proxies
              for aurochs and tarpan, with very different ethical
              outcomes (Oostvaardersplassen's winter mortality
              controversy on one hand, Knepp's publicly celebrated
              regeneration on the other).
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/60 leading-relaxed mt-3">
              <em>What this borrows:</em> the usefulness of
              &ldquo;closest available proxy&rdquo; logic, and the
              hard lesson that welfare protocols have to be built
              in at the start, not bolted on after a winter goes
              badly.<br>
              <em>What this changes:</em> in an arid North American
              system, the proxy, the forage, and the failure modes
              are different; Oostvaardersplassen's lessons apply
              in the welfare framework more than in the species choice.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          The honest positioning: this experiment borrows the
          long-horizon governance of Armendaris, the measurement
          discipline of Pleistocene Park, the landscape aggregation
          intuition of American Prairie, and the welfare lessons
          of Oostvaardersplassen &mdash; and deliberately uses a
          proxy animal and a target loop that none of them test.
          The question this project asks has, as far as I can find,
          not been asked in this form anywhere.<sup><a href="#fn4" id="ref4">4</a></sup>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6: The site -->
      <section id="s06" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">06</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          The site.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The experiment wants a degraded-rangeland parcel in the
          American Southwest or northern Mexico with the following
          characteristics.
        </p>

        <ul class="space-y-3 font-serif text-ink/85 pl-6 list-disc">
          <li>
            <strong>Size.</strong> Five hundred to one thousand
            contiguous acres, fenceable, with enough topographic
            variation to host both shrubland and partial-shade
            hedgerow plantings.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Historic range.</strong> Inside or adjacent to
            documented <em>Camelops</em> occurrence per the
            Paleobiology Database. This is not a romantic
            requirement; it ensures the soil and plant communities
            are ones that once processed a camelid's mouth.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Climate.</strong> Annual precipitation of roughly
            200 to 400 millimeters. Aridland or semi-aridland, not
            true desert; not moist enough to confound the
            desertification signal.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Baseline degradation.</strong> Visible signs of
            current-condition stress &mdash; bare ground above ten
            percent, thinning perennial cover, active erosion, or
            measured declines in soil carbon over the last two
            decades. A site that is already fine will not test the
            hypothesis.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Governance.</strong> A single landholder or a
            small consortium with ten-year-plus commitment horizon.
            Federal grazing lease land is not suitable; too many
            cross-cutting authorities.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Neighbors.</strong> Buffer distance from row-crop
            agriculture sufficient to prevent camel wander into
            planted fields. An adjacent or upstream tribal nation
            should be consulted before any site is selected; no
            site proceeds without consultation.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 7: The herd -->
      <section id="s07" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">07</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          The herd.
        </h2>

        <p>
          One hundred dromedaries (<em>Camelus dromedarius</em>),
          sex ratio approximately 1 male per 10 females, sourced
          from established North American working herds. Dromedaries
          rather than Bactrians for the target climate. The herd
          size is a floor: enough animals that herd behavior is
          real, not enough that the stocking rate exceeds what the
          land can support at the target density. Ten-year carrying
          capacity is recalculated from ground-cover data in years
          three, five, and seven.
        </p>

        <p>
          Animal welfare is not a side consideration. Body condition
          scoring, reproductive success, and mortality are reported
          annually. If any of the three deteriorates beyond protocol
          thresholds, the stocking rate drops. The experiment is
          designed to answer a question about the land. It is not
          designed to demonstrate what camels can be made to endure.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 8: The plantings -->
      <section id="s08" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">08</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          The plantings.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The osage orange orchard is the diagnostic feature and is
          planted at orchard density across a ten- to twenty-acre
          block inside the larger site. Sourced from established
          regional stock. Planted with shade shelters and drip
          irrigation for the first three years to guarantee
          establishment; irrigation then removed to test the
          loop-restoration hypothesis under natural precipitation.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ancillary plantings &mdash; honey locust, Kentucky
          coffeetree, and mesquite &mdash; are established in
          replicated plots across the larger site at multiple
          densities to give a crude dose-response curve. Native
          grasses and forbs are not re-seeded; the hypothesis is
          that the loop, once closed, does some of that work itself.
          If it does not, the null result is informative.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 9: Timeline -->
      <section id="s09" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">09</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          Timeline.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The experiment runs in two committed phases, with a
          defined third phase that is only entered if the second
          signals green. Phase ends are pre-registered go/no-go
          points, not status checkpoints: each one publishes what
          it found, whether or not the next phase is triggered.
        </p>

        <ul class="space-y-4 font-serif text-ink/85 pl-6 list-disc">
          <li>
            <strong>Years 0&ndash;1. Site preparation.</strong>
            Parcel acquired at distressed basis. Baseline surveys
            of soil carbon, soil depth, infiltration, vegetation
            cover, species composition, and invertebrate and
            small-vertebrate communities. Permits secured. Tribal
            consultation completed. Perimeter fencing built. A
            paired control site is identified and contracted.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Years 1&ndash;5. The MVP phase.</strong>
            Camels introduced in two cohorts. Plantings established.
            Irrigation runs for the orchard block Years 1&ndash;3,
            then is removed. Monitoring protocol operates
            continuously. This is the window in which the proximate
            questions are answerable: fruit consumption, gut-passage
            germination, dispersal distance from mother trees,
            early ground-cover and infiltration response, welfare
            stability of the herd. Phase ends with a
            <strong>Year-5 go/no-go</strong> against the
            pre-registered failure criteria (Section 11) and a
            public report, regardless of outcome.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Years 5&ndash;10. The flip window.</strong>
            Only entered if the Year-5 signal is interpretable
            and not-falsifying. This is the phase the Epilogue
            names explicitly: a decade of closed-loop operation
            with honest monitoring, after which the parcel takes
            one of the four defined dispositions set out in
            Section 02. This is the window in which soil-carbon
            change at 0&ndash;30 cm, shifts in species composition,
            and early seedling recruitment away from mother trees
            become detectable.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Years 10&ndash;25. The closed-loop coda.</strong>
            Optional. Only pursued under Disposition B (retained
            and extended), and only because one tree generation
            of osage orange is the single question that cannot
            be answered on a decadal horizon. Whether the
            camel-dispersed seedlings reach reproductive age and
            re-seed the orchard without further camel intervention
            &mdash; this is the final closed-loop diagnostic, and
            it needs fifteen more years than the flip window
            provides.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          The headline commitment is five years. The fundable
          commitment is ten. Twenty-five is the answer to a
          specific tree-generation question that most funders and
          landowners should not be asked to underwrite in advance.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 10: Measurements -->
      <section id="s10" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">10</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          What we would measure.
        </h2>

        <p>
          All measurements are taken against a baseline established
          in Year 0 and against a paired control site in the same
          region that receives no intervention.
        </p>

        <ul class="space-y-3 font-serif text-ink/85 pl-6 list-disc">
          <li>Soil organic carbon, at 0&ndash;10 cm and 10&ndash;30 cm, on a permanent grid.</li>
          <li>Water infiltration rate (double-ring infiltrometer) at permanent stations.</li>
          <li>Bulk density and penetration resistance.</li>
          <li>Perennial ground cover percentage, measured by transect and by aerial imagery.</li>
          <li>Plant species richness and composition.</li>
          <li>Seedling density at varying distances from mother osage orange trees.</li>
          <li>Observed and camera-trapped camel fruit-consumption events.</li>
          <li>Germination-rate comparison: seeds passed through the camel gut versus seeds collected from fallen fruit.</li>
          <li>Dung-deposition mapping and nutrient analysis of dung piles.</li>
          <li>Invertebrate biomass (pitfall, yellow-pan).</li>
          <li>Small-vertebrate occurrence (camera trap, acoustic).</li>
          <li>Near-surface soil temperature and moisture at permanent stations.</li>
          <li>Camel body condition, reproductive success, and mortality.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 11: Failure criteria -->
      <section id="s11" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">11</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          What would tell us the hypothesis is wrong.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The experiment is framed to be falsifiable at two
          pre-registered gates. Failure at either gate triggers
          the defined stop &mdash; publish the null, wind the
          parcel down to its named disposition, and do not roll
          forward to the next phase.
        </p>

        <p class="font-serif small-caps text-xs text-ochre mt-6 mb-2">
          Year-5 gate (MVP phase)
        </p>

        <ul class="space-y-3 font-serif text-ink/85 pl-6 list-disc">
          <li>
            Camels decline or refuse osage orange fruit at
            ecologically meaningful rates after establishment.
          </li>
          <li>
            Seeds passed through the camel gut show equal or lower
            germination rates than fallen-fruit controls across
            two fruiting seasons.
          </li>
          <li>
            Perennial ground cover and infiltration rate show no
            trend toward the control &mdash; or move <em>against</em>
            the hypothesis &mdash; by Year 5.
          </li>
          <li>
            Camel welfare metrics fail protocol thresholds and the
            herd cannot be sustained at ecologically meaningful
            density without supplementary feed.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p class="font-serif small-caps text-xs text-ochre mt-6 mb-2">
          Year-10 gate (flip-window close)
        </p>

        <ul class="space-y-3 font-serif text-ink/85 pl-6 list-disc">
          <li>
            Seedling recruitment away from mother trees is
            statistically indistinguishable from the no-camel
            control site by Year 10.
          </li>
          <li>
            Soil carbon at 0&ndash;30 cm does not increase relative
            to control by Year 10.
          </li>
          <li>
            Perennial ground cover does not increase relative to
            control by Year 10.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          A failed result does not invalidate the broader Pleistocene
          rewilding argument; a single-site failure would constrain
          it. The committed position of the project is that a
          falsifiable failure is more valuable than a cautious
          non-answer.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 12: Objections -->
      <section id="s12" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">12</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          What we hear most, answered.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Six objections surface immediately when a thoughtful
          reader hears the proposal. They are stated here in their
          strongest form, then answered as directly as the project
          can answer them. None of the answers below are the last
          word, and each one invites follow-up from anyone who
          wants to push harder on them.
        </p>

        <div class="space-y-8 not-prose font-body my-8">

          <div class="border-l-2 border-ochre pl-6">
            <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Objection</p>
            <p class="font-display text-xl text-ink mb-3 leading-snug">
              &ldquo;Aren't camels invasive?&rdquo;
            </p>
            <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
              &ldquo;Invasive&rdquo; is a status assigned against a
              reference baseline. The Columbian baseline that
              conservation biology inherited treats camels as
              non-native because they weren't present in 1492. A
              Pleistocene baseline has camelids as continental
              residents for the better part of forty million years,
              absent for the last twelve and a half thousand.<sup><a href="#fn5" id="ref5">5</a></sup>
              The experiment does not claim dromedaries are native;
              it claims they are the closest available proxy for a
              native ecological function that has been missing for
              an evolutionarily brief span. Risk of unintended spread
              is the thing the full perimeter fencing, the 10-year
              welfare and carrying-capacity protocol, and the
              pre-registered site-scale boundaries are designed
              to manage.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border-l-2 border-ochre pl-6">
            <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Objection</p>
            <p class="font-display text-xl text-ink mb-3 leading-snug">
              &ldquo;What about disease?&rdquo;
            </p>
            <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
              Camel-to-livestock and camel-to-wildlife pathogen
              transmission is a real concern, most seriously around
              MERS-CoV, brucellosis, and a small set of parasitic
              loads. USDA APHIS clearance at import and animal
              movement, a closed herd after establishment, quarterly
              veterinary screening, and siting rules that put the
              herd at defensible distance from adjacent livestock
              are the answer in protocol form. In practice: the
              herd is sourced from established North American
              working dromedary stock that has already cleared
              these screens, not from imported animals, which
              reduces the incoming-pathogen surface area
              considerably.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border-l-2 border-ochre pl-6">
            <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Objection</p>
            <p class="font-display text-xl text-ink mb-3 leading-snug">
              &ldquo;What about water? In a drying climate?&rdquo;
            </p>
            <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
              Dromedaries' water requirements are substantially
              below those of cattle at comparable body mass &mdash;
              on the order of 30 to 60 litres per animal per week
              under the target climate, drawn from existing surface
              water or low-volume supplemental. The herd's water
              footprint at 100 head is under what the site would
              already supply to a marginal cow-calf operation at
              the same stocking rate by body mass. If the hypothesis
              is right and infiltration improves, site-scale water
              availability should increase over the measurement
              window, not decrease. If infiltration does not improve,
              that is part of the failure signal.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border-l-2 border-ochre pl-6">
            <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Objection</p>
            <p class="font-display text-xl text-ink mb-3 leading-snug">
              &ldquo;What about welfare &mdash; winter, predators,
              boredom, injury?&rdquo;
            </p>
            <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
              Welfare is protocol, not afterthought. Body condition
              scoring, reproductive success, and mortality are
              reported annually against pre-specified thresholds;
              failure of any of the three triggers stocking-rate
              reduction or supplemental feed. The lesson of
              Oostvaardersplassen is that welfare thresholds
              announced at the start are less painful for everyone,
              including the animals, than thresholds negotiated
              during a bad winter. The target climate is inside
              the dromedary's established tolerance range; predator
              pressure in the target region is primarily coyote
              and does not typically threaten adult camelids.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border-l-2 border-ochre pl-6">
            <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Objection</p>
            <p class="font-display text-xl text-ink mb-3 leading-snug">
              &ldquo;Isn't it ethically fraught to introduce a
              species for ecological engineering?&rdquo;
            </p>
            <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
              The project treats this objection as real and
              unresolved, not as pushed past. The experiment is
              not an introduction at continental or even ecoregional
              scale; it is a fenced, monitored, time-boxed site.
              Tribal consultation is a hard prerequisite, not a
              courtesy, and any site in proximity to a tribal
              nation does not proceed without active partnership.
              The ethical objection that cannot be answered by
              protocol &mdash; that this kind of engineering is
              the wrong stance to take toward a landscape at all
              &mdash; is acknowledged, and it is one of the reasons
              the outcome measure includes whether the land
              eventually stops needing intervention.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border-l-2 border-ochre pl-6">
            <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Objection</p>
            <p class="font-display text-xl text-ink mb-3 leading-snug">
              &ldquo;Why not just use bison, or cattle, or nothing
              at all?&rdquo;
            </p>
            <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
              Bison-led grassland restoration is a real and
              partially-tested intervention and is what American
              Prairie is running. It tests a different hypothesis:
              a post-glacial grazer restoring a post-glacial
              grassland. The question this experiment asks is
              whether a browser proxy at body mass can reopen a
              specific plant&ndash;disperser loop that pre-dates
              bison. Cattle are a poor proxy for the browsing
              function in question; their mouth, gut, and behavior
              don't do the job the missing mouth did. &ldquo;Nothing
              at all&rdquo; is the status quo, and the status quo
              is the control site. The experiment is, precisely,
              the question of whether the status quo is the best
              available answer.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p class="font-serif italic text-ink/70">
          If none of these answers is yours and you have a harder
          one, send it. The experiment gets stronger by hearing
          objections sharper than the ones on this page.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 13: What's being asked -->
      <section id="s13" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">13</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          What is being asked.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The ask is structured to match the phase structure above.
          Each phase names its own capital, commitment, and exit.
          Nothing in Phase 2 is asked of anyone in Phase 1 who does
          not want to continue.
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 not-prose my-8 font-body">
          <div class="border border-ink/15 p-6 bg-bone/30">
            <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Phase 1 &mdash; MVP (Y0&ndash;5)</div>
            <div class="font-display text-lg mb-3">Acquire, establish, answer.</div>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              <strong>Land.</strong> 500&ndash;1,000 contiguous
              acres meeting the site characteristics in Section 06.
              <em>Acquired at distressed valuation</em>, not
              donated. Fee-simple or lease-to-purchase.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              <strong>Capital.</strong> Roughly US$3.5&ndash;4.5M
              all-in: acquisition, perimeter fencing, herd purchase
              and transport, orchard and ancillary plantings, the
              Year-0 baseline, and five years of staffing,
              veterinary care, and monitoring.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Commitment.</strong> Five years. A single,
              bounded research horizon that ends with a go/no-go
              and a public report, regardless of outcome.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-ink/15 p-6 bg-bone/30">
            <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Phase 2 &mdash; Flip (Y5&ndash;10)</div>
            <div class="font-display text-lg mb-3">Hold, measure, dispose.</div>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              <strong>Trigger.</strong> Year-5 data
              not-falsifying against the pre-registered criteria
              (Section 11). Independent replication of the MVP
              at a second site is welcomed and sought.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              <strong>Capital.</strong> Additional
              US$1.5&ndash;2M for five years of continued
              operations, monitoring, and disposition preparation.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/75 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Exit.</strong> At Year 10 the parcel takes
              one of the four named dispositions (Section 02):
              sold at restored valuation, retained through Year
              25, easement-protected, or returned to the commons.
              The disposition is committed at Year 7 to give the
              market time to price the signal in.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>Two asks, independent of phase:</p>

        <ul class="space-y-4 font-serif text-ink/85 pl-6 list-disc">
          <li>
            <strong>Permissions.</strong> USDA APHIS clearance
            for the herd; state agricultural permitting;
            IACUC-equivalent animal welfare oversight; tribal
            consultation where relevant; IRB if the project
            takes on any human-subject research around adjacent
            communities.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A committee that holds back.</strong> The
            single most commonly failed condition of
            long-duration ecological experiments is a governance
            body that cannot leave the site alone. A commitment
            not to intervene during either measurement window is
            asked of every funding and land-holding party.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p class="font-serif italic text-ink/70 mt-6">
          The distinction between <em>patron</em> and
          <em>underwriter</em> matters here. A patron donates;
          an underwriter buys a distressed parcel and sells,
          keeps, or transfers it at a defined exit. Both are
          welcome. The proposal is written so that either can fit.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 14: What this is not -->
      <section id="s14" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">14</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          What this is not.
        </h2>

        <p>
          It is not agritourism. It is not a petting zoo. It is not
          a rewilding demonstration project in the marketing sense
          of that phrase; there is no ribbon-cutting and no
          donor-tier plaque on a camel. It is not a program of the
          Paleo Foundation or any certification program and will
          not be branded as one. It is not a Pleistocene Park sister
          site; Pleistocene Park is a different experiment, on a
          different continent, at different latitude, testing a
          related but distinct hypothesis. And it is not a one-shot
          proof; the null result is informative, and the protocol
          is designed to produce data whether the hypothesis holds
          or does not.
        </p>
      </section>

